    {
      "type": "p",
      "html": "After exact trace reproduction, we compare paired loss differences using Diebold–Mariano (1995) statistics with Bartlett Newey–West (1987) covariance. The fixed bandwidth is L=max{H,⌊4(n/100)<super>2/9</super>⌋}; doubled bandwidth supplies a sensitivity check. Harvey, Leybourne and Newbold (1997) supply the reported small-sample multiplier, here used as an approximation for pinball loss. Fixed finite estimation windows motivate the unconditional predictive-ability interpretation of Giacomini and White (2006), under mixing, adequate high-order moments and positive long-run variance. Shared training can extend dependence beyond the target horizon. These are working assumptions for this post-result analysis.",
      "inline_math": [
        {
          "html": "L=max{H,⌊4(n/100)<super>2/9</super>⌋}",
          "latex": "L=\\max\\{H,\\lfloor4(n/100)^{2/9}\\rfloor\\}"
        }
      ],
